---
import AboutHeroImage from '../assets/blog-placeholder-about.jpg';
import Layout from '../layouts/BlogPost.astro';
---

<Layout
	title="About Rigged Yak"
	description="Our testing methodology, editorial standards, and commitment to independent gear testing."
	pubDate={new Date('January 01 2026')}
	heroImage={AboutHeroImage}
>
	<h2>Our Mission: Real Water Testing</h2>
	<p>
		Founded by dedicated tournament anglers and weekend warriors alike, <strong>Rigged Yak</strong> was created to cut through generic sponsored listicles. We test pedal drives, fish finders, lithium battery setups, and hulls where they belong: out on the water in real currents, wind, and tournament conditions.
	</p>

	<h2>How We Evaluate Fishing Kayaks</h2>
	<p>
		Every kayak we review is evaluated across four strict benchmarks:
	</p>
	<ul>
		<li><strong>Primary & Secondary Stability:</strong> Can an angler comfortably stand, sight-cast, and land large fish without tipping?</li>
		<li><strong>Drive Efficiency & Reliability:</strong> How smoothly does the pedal drive or motor cruise over long distances, and how easy is it to clear hydrilla and shallow snags?</li>
		<li><strong>Rigging Modularity:</strong> Are gear tracks, transducer scuppers, and battery hatches logically placed for modern electronics?</li>
		<li><strong>Portability & Weight:</strong> Is the hull manageable for cart-topping, bed extenders, or trailering?</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>Editorial Independence & Affiliate Transparency</h2>
	<p>
		We believe in 100% transparency. When you purchase through links on our site, we may earn an affiliate commission from retailer partners like Amazon, Bass Pro Shops, or Cabela's at zero additional cost to you. 
	</p>
	<p>
		We never accept payment in exchange for positive reviews or score adjustments. If a kayak has poor tracking, uncomfortable seats, or fragile rudder cables, we say so plainly.
	</p>
</Layout>
